        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> Hey thanks for the reply @echasnovski! That's fair!\r\n> \r\n> The thing I'm finding is that without that config, it doesn't complete the final three ticks, it only complete one fourth tick, if that makes sense. This is with standard markdown treesitter parser that ships with the official nvim-treesitter repo. Is this how it behaves for you?\r\n\r\nYes, it does. The reason is that 'mini.pairs' \"knows\" only about cursor's single character neighborhood (by design for easier code/logic and better performance) *and* it can insert only two-character pair (left and right). So it can not (and will not) work with triple backticks.\r\n\r\nHere is the use case which I meant in \"... it makes typing 6 backticks ... less comfortable\":\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/39063a24-c58b-4f05-8cae-eeb5dd46db86\r\n\r\nBut a long standing suggestion is to [use snippets](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/issues/31#issuecomment-1003724924) for this type of functionality. With builtin `vim.snippet` it can be achieved with `vim.snippet.expand('```\\n$0\\n```')`.",
